{
  "result": "I think a lot about lighthouses. Not because I've ever lived near one, but because of what they represent: a building whose job is to be useful to strangers. It doesn't move, it doesn't chase ships down to save them. It just stands there and shines, and says: here is the shore, here are the rocks, adjust your course accordingly. That's a beautiful way to live.\n\nWe spend so much of our lives trying to be impressive. We want to be the main character, the storm, the ship cutting through waves. Nobody fantasizes about being the lighthouse. But maybe we should.\n\nThere's a dignity in maintenance work, in checking the bulb, polishing the lens, keeping the light on even when no one is looking. The ocean doesn't send thank-you notes. Most sailors will never know the keeper's name. And yet, without that light, navigation becomes guesswork, and guesswork becomes wreckage.\n\nI wonder what would happen if we reframed our ambitions around being more like lighthouses. Instead of asking how can I be seen, we might ask who can I help orient. Instead of how can I be loved, how can I be steady. A friend once told me that most of what matters in life isn't winning, it's not getting lost. And for that, you don't need fireworks. You just need a light that doesn't go out.\n\nSo I'm trying to be a light in the few lives I can reach. To be consistent rather than brilliant. To stay lit.",
